1. What is an intersection type in TypeScript?

An intersection type combines multiple types into a single type using the & operator. The resulting type must satisfy all the combined types.

type A = { name: string };
type B = { age: number };

type User = A & B;

User requires both name and age.

2. What is the difference between union and intersection types?

In TypeScript, union (|) and intersection (&) types are used to combine multiple types. A union type works like an OR operator, meaning a value can 
satisfy either type. For example, type ID = string | number allows a value to be either a string or a number. On the other hand, an intersection 
type works like an AND operator, meaning a value must satisfy all combined types. For example, type Staff = User & Employee requires an object to have 
all the properties of both User and Employee.


3. Can we combine an existing type with an inline object type?

Yes. We don't need to create a separate type alias for every object structure.

type UserResponse = ApiResponse & {
    data: UserData;
};

This combines the properties of ApiResponse with the inline object type.

4. Can we combine more than two types using &?

Yes. We can combine as many compatible types as needed.

type Employee = User & Department & Salary;

An Employee must satisfy all three types.

5. What happens if two intersected types have conflicting properties?

TypeScript combines their requirements. If a property must simultaneously be two incompatible types, such as string and number, its type becomes never.

type A = { id: string };
type B = { id: number };

type C = A & B;

No ordinary object can satisfy C because its id would need to be both a string and a number.

6. What is the difference between using named types and inline object types with &?

Both approaches can produce exactly the same result.

Named types:

type DataResponse = {
    data: UserData;
};

type UserResponse = ApiResponse & DataResponse;

Inline object type:

type UserResponse = ApiResponse & {
    data: UserData;
};

Named types are useful when you want to reuse the same structure elsewhere.

7. Can we use intersection types with interfaces?

Yes. We can combine interfaces using a type alias and the & operator.

interface User {
    name: string;
}

interface Admin {
    permissions: string[];
}

type AdminUser = User & Admin;

AdminUser requires both name and permissions.

8. What is a real-world use case for intersection types?

Intersection types are useful when extending shared data structures, such as API responses.

type ApiResponse = {
    status: number;
    message: string;
};

type UserData = {
    id: number;
    name: string;
};

type UserResponse = ApiResponse & {
    data: UserData;
};

The resulting response contains status, message and data.
